But the roles of the keys in this context are not text or punctuation,
they're just positions.  These programmers must have had easily jarred
minds ;P

>> "jkl;" is still a "convenient cluster together" a la vi, it's just one
>> that doesn't require moving any fingers off their home-position.  (for
>> orthogonal movements, anyway).
>
> Yes, it's a convenient cluster.  But I don't think that it's either as
> easy to type as hjkl for a touch typist,

Why on earth not?  Your fingers are right there!  A real touch typist
should not be "pinky-challenged", injuries aside.  I don't find words that
use, say, 'p' or 'a' any more difficult to type than any other words.

It just seems counter intuitive to me to assign a block of four adjacent
keys to movement, and then access them with only three fingers.  If I were
restricting myself to letters, I would probably choose fghj or dfjk for
orthogonal movements, and tybn for the diagonals, and I would make 't'
correspond to up-right and 'y' to up-left to mirror the direction my
fingers move to press the keys (and similarly for the lower diagonals).

> or consistent with the way programmers used editors back when vi came to
> be.

I have no idea what you mean by this.  In any case, I'm not particularly
interested in the historical reasons for the choice unless they still apply
today.

